# Response headers for the static frontend, read by Cloudflare Workers static
# assets (the `[assets]` binding in wrangler.toml). Vite copies this file from
# frontend/public/ into docs/, the Worker's assets directory; Workers parses it
# at upload and applies the rules to the responses the assets binding serves —
# the SPA shell on every path outside `run_worker_first` (`/login`,
# `/dashboard`, the single-page-application fallback for every deep link).
# The file itself is never served.
#
# Restored 2026-09-03 (documentation/architecture/DECISIONS.md D36) from the
# copy #371 deleted on 2026-08-31, when a Pages Advanced Mode `_worker.js`
# beside it made it inert. That script, and the Cloudflare Pages mirror it ran
# on, are retired; this file is the only thing setting headers on the shell.
#
# NOT applied to responses the Worker script generates — the
# `run_worker_first` paths `/api/*`, `/landing/*`, `/p/*` and `/assets/*`.
# The first three get cloudflare-worker/src/middleware/securityHeaders.ts;
# `/assets/*` is answered by cloudflare-worker/src/index.ts straight from the
# assets binding, which is why there is no `/assets/*` Cache-Control rule
# here — it would be inert.
#
# Values are the Worker middleware's own (securityHeaders.ts).
# frontend/test/apex_truth_doc.test.mjs fails if the two drift apart, and
# scripts/check-spa-live.mjs asserts these headers on every live shell route
# after a deploy — GOTCHAS records this surface losing its headers twice while
# documents said it had them.
#
# Referrer-Policy is deliberately the laxer value here. The Worker's
# authenticated API surface uses `no-referrer` (NICE-SEC-01) because its URLs
# carry ids and query params; these static pages carry neither and benefit
# from cross-origin referral attribution. The split is intentional — see
# GOTCHAS, "Referrer-Policy is two-tier". Do not unify them.
/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=(), bluetooth=(), accelerometer=(), gyroscope=(), magnetometer=(), midi=(), serial=(), hid=(), interest-cohort=()

# NO Content-Security-Policy here, and that is a decision rather than an
# omission. The Worker's CSP is nonce-based and generated per request, which a
# static header file cannot reproduce. index.html carries an inline boot
# watchdog, so a naive `script-src 'self'` would break the app on load, and a
# wrong CSP on the frontend is a white screen for every visitor. The static
# shell has never carried a CSP, so shipping without one is not a regression.
# Adding a correct static CSP needs the inline scripts enumerated and hashed
# against a deployed preview.
